## **Best Follow-Up: Recording Interface (ADC/DAC)**

**Why this makes sense after MIDI:**

1. **Natural skill escalation**
   - MIDI taught you: microcontroller I/O, serial communication, basic UX
   - Recording interface teaches: analog signal conditioning, power supply design, real-time performance
   - You're building on what you learned, not starting from scratch

2. **Bridges electronics and audio**
   - Forces you to understand "Practical Electronics for Inventors" chapters on op-amps and signal chains
   - You'll learn why clean power matters (audio is unforgiving)
   - Directly applicable to all other interface types

3. **Design complexity sweet spot**
   - More challenging than MIDI (analog circuitry, impedance, noise)
   - Less overwhelming than synthesizers or effects (no DSP algorithms needed)
   - You can build a functional 2-channel interface in a weekend

4. **Immediate practical use**
   - You can actually use it to record music right away
   - Works with your DAW and existing gear
   - Gives you credibility with musicians

5. **Foundation for everything else**
   - **→ Effects Processor:** Add DSP to process the audio you're capturing
   - **→ Synthesizer:** Add DSP to generate instead of capture
   - **→ Analyzer:** Strip out the output and add measurement firmware
   - **→ Hybrid:** Combine multiple of these skills

